<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Left</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1960s–1970s</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The New Left was a broad political movement of activists in the Western world that emerged from the counterculture of the 1960s and continued through the 1970s. It campaigned on social issues such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feminism">feminism</a>, gay rights, drug policy reform and gender relations, where the traditional left had put economic goals first. The sociologist C. Wright Mills popularised the term in a 1960 open letter, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> New Left Review was founded the same year. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the movement centred on campus protest, above all the Students for a Democratic Society, whose 1962 Port Huron Statement called for participatory democracy, and on opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a>; by 1968 it had begun to split.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the end of the Second World War the movement had been severely weakened, but it revived in the 1960s in the anti-nuclear, environmental and peace movements, the counterculture and the <b>New Left</b>, and later in punk, anarcha-feminism and black anarchism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>By 1968 the Vietnam War had given rise to the <b>New Left</b>, critical of both the Soviet Union and social democracy.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/socialism">Socialism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
